{"name":"Twisted Tail","hex":"#9a845e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"twisted","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Consisting of two or more threads, strands or the like intertwined; formed by twisting or twining. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Mentally or emotionally distorted or unsound; perverted."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of twist"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/twisted"},{"term":"tail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The caudal appendage of an animal that is attached to their posterior and near the anus or cloaca.","An object or part of an object resembling a tail in shape, such as the thongs on a cat-o'-nine-tails; a strand of material hanging from something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hold by the end; said of a timber when it rests upon a wall or other support; with in or into","To swing with the stern in a certain direction; said of a vessel at anchor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tail","blocks":[{"text":"The tail is the elongated section at the rear end of a bilaterian animal's body; in general, the term refers to a distinct, flexible appendage extending backwards from the midline of the torso. In vertebrate animals that evolved to lose their tails, the coccyx is the homologous vestigial of the tail. While tails are primarily considered a feature of vertebrates, some invertebrates such as scorpions and springtails, as well as snails and slugs, have tail-like appendages that are also referred to as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg/330px-White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Alopex_lagopus_IMG_9019.JPG/500px-Alopex_lagopus_IMG_9019.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Alopex_lagopus_IMG_9019.JPG/1280px-Alopex_lagopus_IMG_9019.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Alopex_lagopus_IMG_9019.JPG/500px-Alopex_lagopus_IMG_9019.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vulpes lagopus (Arctic fox) sleeping with its tail wrapped as a blanket.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlopex_lagopus_IMG_9019.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Gould_Pyle_129.jpg/500px-Gould_Pyle_129.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Gould_Pyle_129.jpg/500px-Gould_Pyle_129.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tail-like structure on a female newborn from coccyx protrusion","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGould_Pyle_129.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cub_Stalks_Tail.jpg/120px-Cub_Stalks_Tail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cub_Stalks_Tail.jpg/250px-Cub_Stalks_Tail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cub_Stalks_Tail.jpg/120px-Cub_Stalks_Tail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACub_Stalks_Tail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Scorpion_tail.jpg/120px-Scorpion_tail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/t